WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.449
Film Courage: Novel structure versus screenplay
structure?

00:00:02.449 --> 00:00:03.449
How are they different?

00:00:03.449 --> 00:00:04.449
How are they the same?

00:00:04.449 --> 00:00:07.520
Jennifer Brody: I think that there is a lot
of similarities between them.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.099
When I teach writing I tend to actually reference
screenplay structure.

00:00:12.099 --> 00:00:17.381
The difference would be that screenplays are
much more rigid in terms of the First Act

00:00:17.381 --> 00:00:23.830
which is usually the first 30 pages or so,
the Second Act, Midpoint and then how long

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:24.830
a script can be.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:28.180
It is much more rigid in terms of the way
they’re structured.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.310
Where with the book you have more leeway.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.510
The word count can vary.

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:33.840
The page count can vary.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.390
And then, exactly when you are hitting these
big moments can also vary.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:42.040
Novels can also get away with being more episodic.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:47.150
Which is to say one thing happens, one thing
happens, one thing happens, it’s not always

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:48.150
building.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:49.930
Whereas in a film you can’t really do that.

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:54.610
Or if you do, I think you are a lot of times
in trouble because it’s not really building

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:56.110
into something that’s a climax.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:01:00.550
With most films you are looking at 90 minutes,
2 hours really at the most.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.540
I know some films go longer but usually it
doesn’t work out well.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.750
I think you just have a lot more leeway.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:11.939
But that said I really believe that books
benefit from having a lot of the same things

00:01:11.939 --> 00:01:13.200
in place.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.340
Instead of in the First act moment, I would
call it the Big Thing That Happens Right Away

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:20.859
or the Inciting Incident of a book.

00:01:20.859 --> 00:01:24.270
That would really be the end of a first act
in a screenplay.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:29.189
We use a little bit of a different terminology
in terms of what I would call it.

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:33.159
But like I said, I think with novels you can
be a little more flexible.

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:39.300
I tend to think that having the same things,
climax, moral choice of the character, and

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.729
then after the climax, resolution…I mean
those things are all the same for the most

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:44.729
part.

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:46.490
Stories are told a certain way for a reason.

